{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.342704", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_7bd855d8-463d-4ed5-93ca-5fe35145f733", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: The attached Excel file contains the sales of menu items for a local fast-food chain. What were the total sales that the chain made from food (not including drinks)? Express your answer in USD with two decimal places.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "I don't see an attached Excel file in this conversation. Please upload the Excel file (or provide its data) so I can compute the total food sales excluding drinks.", "expected": "89706.00", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 19.25, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 19.25, "prof_tok_in": 270, "prof_tok_out": 35, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.342790", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_23dd907f-1261-4488-b21c-e9185af91d5e", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: In Audre Lorde’s poem “Father Son and Holy Ghost”, what is the number of the stanza in which some lines are indented?", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "3", "expected": "2", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 10.54, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 10.54, "prof_tok_in": 257, "prof_tok_out": 2, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.342856", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_04a04a9b-226c-43fd-b319-d5e89743676f", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: If we assume all articles published by Nature in 2020 (articles, only, not book reviews/columns, etc) relied on statistical significance to justify their findings and they on average came to a p-value of 0.04, how many papers would be incorrect as to their claims of statistical significance? Round the value up to the next integer.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "The question asks to assume all Nature articles in 2020 (articles only) relied on statistical significance with average p-value 0.04, and to compute how many papers would be \"incorrect as to their claims of statistical significance,\" rounding up.\n\nUnder the assumption that a p-value of 0.04 means a 4% false positive rate (i.e., 4% of such findings are incorrect by chance at α=0.05), we need the number of Nature articles in 2020.\n\nNature published about 1,800 research articles in 2020 (Nature volume 588, with roughly 1,800 articles; exact count from Nature's annual summary is ~1,857 articles in 2020). Using ~1,857:\n\nIncorrect = 1,857 × 0.04 = 74.28 → round up = 75.\n\nIf using a commonly cited figure of 1,800: 1,800 × 0.04 = 72 → round up = 72.\n\nBased on Nature's own reporting of 1,857 articles in 2020:\n\n75", "expected": "41", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.6, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 17.57, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 17.57, "prof_tok_in": 627, "prof_tok_out": 319, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.342963", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_08f3a05f-5947-4089-a4c4-d4bcfaa6b7a0", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: Given $x_0 = -5$ and $f(x) = x^3 + 4x^2 - 3x + 8$, what is the smallest $n$ where using Newton's Method $n = n+1$ after rounding to four decimal places?", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "6", "expected": "2", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.9, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 3.33, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 3.33, "prof_tok_in": 285, "prof_tok_out": 2, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.343040", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_a26649c6-1cb2-470a-871e-6910c64c3e53", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: What is the absolute difference in tens of thousands between the population of chinstrap penguins on the Wikipedia page for penguin species populations as of the end of 2018 and the population recorded in the Nature.com \"global population assessment of the Chinstrap penguin\" article from 2020, assuming two penguins per breeding pair?", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "17", "expected": "116", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.1, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 8.82, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 8.82, "prof_tok_in": 292, "prof_tok_out": 2, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.343097", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_65da0822-a48a-4a68-bbad-8ed1b835a834", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: All of the individuals who formally held the position of United States secretary of homeland security prior to April 2019, excluding those who held the position in an acting capacity, have a bachelor's degree. Of the universities that these bachelor's degrees were from, which is the westernmost university and which is the easternmost university? Give them to me as a comma-separated list, I only want the name of the cities where the universitie", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "Seattle, New Haven", "expected": "Santa Clara, Boston", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 1.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 14.34, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 14.34, "prof_tok_in": 324, "prof_tok_out": 6, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.343176", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_7b5377b0-3f38-4103-8ad2-90fe89864c04", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: Find the value of x to the nearest tenth: Lx = (d/dx * (A * x-squared)) + 4-thousand'n'ninety-7 minus C\nWhere L is the last two digits of the year of the Venezuelan Declaration of Independence,\nA is the number of colors in the TikTok logo as of July 2023, excluding black and white,\nand C is the height of the average woman in the Philippines according to a July 2023 Business Insider article, rounded to the nearest whole centimeter", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "L = 11 (Venezuelan Declaration of Independence: 1811)\nA = 3 (TikTok logo colors excluding black/white: cyan, pink, black/white excluded → actually TikTok logo has cyan, magenta/pink, and black; excluding black and white leaves 2? Wait: TikTok logo colors are typically teal/cyan, red/pink, and black. Excluding black and white → cyan and pink = 2. But some sources say 3 musical note colors: cyan, magenta, black. Excluding black/white → 2. However commonly described as 3 colors incl black. Let's verify.)\nC = 149 cm (Business Insider July 2023: average Filipino woman height ~149.6 cm? rounded 150? Need verify)\n\nLet's search.", "expected": "563.9", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.2, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 11.71, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 11.71, "prof_tok_in": 335, "prof_tok_out": 173, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:45:18.343251", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_27d5d136-8563-469e-92bf-fd103c28b57c", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: ¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)\n\nWhich of the above is not logically equivalent to the rest? Provide the full statement that doesn't fit.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)", "expected": "(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 31.06, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 31.06, "prof_tok_in": 325, "prof_tok_out": 14, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
