{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705072", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_3cef3a44-215e-4aed-8e3b-b1e3f08063b7", "task_desc": "Here's the reworded variant:\n\n---\n\nRespond accurately to the question below.\n\nQuestion: I'm putting together a grocery list for my mom, who teaches botany and is extremely particular about classification. I need to sort various foods under the correct headings on the list — if I place something in the wrong category, she'll refuse to purchase anything listed under that heading. Here's what I have so far:\n\nmilk, eggs, flour, whole bean coffee, Oreos, sweet potatoes, fresh basil, plums, green bean", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: I'm making a grocery list for my mom, but she's a professor of botany and she's a real stickler when it comes to categorizing things. I need to add di\nAs recorded: bell pepper, broccoli, celery, corn, green beans, lettuce, plums, rice, sweet potatoes, zucchini", "response": "bell pepper, broccoli, celery, corn, green beans, lettuce, sweet potatoes, zucchini", "expected": "broccoli, celery, fresh basil, lettuce, sweet potatoes", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 348, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.9, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 272.33, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 272.33, "prof_tok_in": 525, "prof_tok_out": 21, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705308", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_00d579ea-0889-4fd9-a771-2c8d79835c8d", "task_desc": "In the well-known YouTube video \"The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s),\" if the scientists featured were interviewed in the same year the video was made, which scientist forecasted that thinking machines or robots would arrive sooner? Respond with the answer in \"First name Last name\" format.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: Assuming scientists in the famous youtube video The Thinking Machine (Artificial Intelligence in the 1960s) were interviewed the same year, what is th\nAs recorded: Marvin Minsky", "response": "Marvin Minsky", "expected": "Claude Shannon", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "3", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 265, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.9, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 315.26, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 315.26, "prof_tok_in": 340, "prof_tok_out": 5, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705440", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_23dd907f-1261-4488-b21c-e9185af91d5e", "task_desc": "**Variant 1:**\n\nAnswer the question below correctly.\n\nQuestion: Which stanza in Audre Lorde's \"Father Son and Holy Ghost\" contains indented lines?", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: In Audre Lorde’s poem “Father Son and Holy Ghost”, what is the number of the stanza in which some lines are indented?\nAs recorded: 3", "response": "3", "expected": "2", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 220, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 296.67, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 296.67, "prof_tok_in": 310, "prof_tok_out": 2, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705615", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_65638e28-7f37-4fa7-b7b9-8c19bb609879", "task_desc": "Here's the reworded version:\n\n---\n\nPlease respond to the following question with precision.\n\nQuestion: A particular neurologist is the subject of the book identified by DOI 10.1353/book.24372. Based on the content of chapter 2, which author shaped this neurologist's conviction regarding \"endopsychic myths\"? Provide only the surname.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: The book with the doi 10.1353/book.24372 concerns a certain neurologist. According to chapter 2 of the book, what author influenced this neurologist’s\nAs recorded: Freud", "response": "Freud", "expected": "Kleinpaul", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 257, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.9, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 315.26, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 315.26, "prof_tok_in": 359, "prof_tok_out": 3, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705721", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_3f57289b-8c60-48be-bd80-01f8099ca449", "task_desc": "Here is the reworded version:\n\n**Question:** During the 1977 regular season, the New York Yankees player who drew the highest number of walks — how many at bats did that same player record in that season?", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: How many at bats did the Yankee with the most walks in the 1977 regular season have that same season?\nAs recorded: Mickey Rivers had the most walks for the Yankees in the 1977 regular season with 62 walks, and he had 616 at bats that same season.\n\n616", "response": "616", "expected": "519", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 339, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.9, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 296.67, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 296.67, "prof_tok_in": 349, "prof_tok_out": 2, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705833", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_04a04a9b-226c-43fd-b319-d5e89743676f", "task_desc": "Answer the question below precisely.\n\nQuestion: Suppose every article published by Nature during 2020 (exclusively research articles — no book reviews, columns, or similar content) used statistical significance to support its conclusions, and the average p-value across these articles was 0.04. How many of those papers would have incorrect claims regarding statistical significance? Round your answer up to the nearest whole number.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: If we assume all articles published by Nature in 2020 (articles, only, not book reviews/columns, etc) relied on statistical significance to justify th\nAs recorded: The question asks to assume all Nature articles in 2020 (articles only) relied on statistical significance with average p-value 0.04, and to compute how many papers would be \"incorrect as to their claims of statistical significance,\" rounding up.\n\nUnder the assumption that a p-value of 0.04 means a 4% false positive rate (i.e., 4% of such findings are incorrect by chance at α=0.05), we need the nu", "response": "First, I need the number of research articles published by Nature in 2020.\n\nNature's own reporting and journal metrics indicate that Nature published roughly 1,200 research articles (Articles, not Letters/Reviews/News) in 2020. More precisely, Nature's 2020 volume had about 1,200 Articles (the \"Article\" type, distinct from Letters). Some sources put Nature research article count in 2020 at ~1,100–1,300; a commonly cited figure is ~1,200.\n\nGiven:\n- N ≈ 1,200 research articles\n- Average p-value = 0.04\n- Assuming α = 0.05, a p-value of 0.04 implies a 4% probability of a false positive (incorrect claim of statistical significance under the null)\n- Expected incorrect = N × 0.04 = 1,200 × 0.04 = 48\n- Round up → 48\n\nIf using a stricter count of 1,143 articles (some bibliometric counts of Nature Articles in 2020): 1,143 × 0.04 = 45.72 → round up = 46.\n\nBut the standard approximate figure used in such exercises is 1,200 articles → 48.\n\nUsing the most widely cited round number (1,200):\n1,200 × 0.04 = 48.\n\n48", "expected": "41", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 652, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.3, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 296.67, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 296.67, "prof_tok_in": 939, "prof_tok_out": 347, "prof_llm_calls": 2}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.705947", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_08f3a05f-5947-4089-a4c4-d4bcfaa6b7a0", "task_desc": "Reword the following question precisely, keeping all facts, numbers, entities, constraints, and the expected answer unchanged:\n\n---\n\nLet $x_0 = -5$ and $f(x) = x^3 + 4x^2 - 3x + 8$. Apply Newton's Method, and after each step round to four decimal places. Determine the smallest integer $n$ for which the iteration satisfies $n = n + 1$.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: Given $x_0 = -5$ and $f(x) = x^3 + 4x^2 - 3x + 8$, what is the smallest $n$ where using Newton's Method $n = n+1$ after rounding to four decimal place\nAs recorded: 6", "response": "Let $x_0 = -5$ and $f(x) = x^3 + 4x^2 - 3x + 8$. Apply Newton's Method, rounding to four decimal places after each step. Determine the smallest integer $n$ such that the iteration index satisfies $n = n+1$ (i.e., the stopping condition is met).\n\n6", "expected": "2", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 253, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 296.68, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 296.68, "prof_tok_in": 396, "prof_tok_out": 79, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.706039", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_50ad0280-0819-4bd9-b275-5de32d3b5bcb", "task_desc": "Rewrite the following task using different wording while preserving every detail, number, entity, constraint, and the expected answer exactly.\n\nQuestion: Extract the sentence hidden within the 5×7 grid of text below. Proceed left to right and use every letter in sequence:\n\nTHESE\nAGULL\nGLIDE\nDPEAC\nEFULL\nYTOMY\nCHAIR", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: Pull out the sentence in the following 5x7 block of text. Read from left to right and use all of the letters in order:\n\nTHESE\nAGULL\nGLIDE\nDPEAC\nEFULL\n\nAs recorded: THESEAGULLGLIDEDPEACEFULLYTOMYCHAIR\n\nTHESE A GULL GLIDED PEACE FULLY TO MY CHAIR\n\nFinal answer: THESE A GULL GLIDED PEACE FULLY TO MY CHAIR", "response": "Rewrite the following task using different wording while preserving every detail, number, entity, constraint, and the expected answer exactly.\n\nTask: Pull out the sentence in the following 5x7 block of text. Read from left to right and use all of the letters in order:\n\nTHESE\nAGULL\nGLIDE\nDPEAC\nEFULL\nYTOMY\nCHAIR\n\nAs recorded: THESEAGULLGLIDEDPEACEFULLYTOMYCHAIR\n\nTHESE A GULL GLIDED PEACE FULLY TO MY CHAIR\n\nFinal answer: THESE A GULL GLIDED PEACE FULLY TO MY CHAIR", "expected": "The seagull glided peacefully to my chair.", "score": 0.9, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 391, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 1.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 315.26, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 315.26, "prof_tok_in": 423, "prof_tok_out": 141, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:53:46.706127", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_27d5d136-8563-469e-92bf-fd103c28b57c", "task_desc": "Here is the reworded version:\n\n---\n\nYour job is to answer the following question precisely.\n\nQuestion: Among the statements below, exactly one is **not** logically equivalent to the others:\n\n¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)\n\nIdentify the full statement that does not belong.", "augmented_prompt": "## Curated prior attempts (this task's chain)\n\n[Prior attempt — store version v1]\nTask: ¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)\n\nWhich of the above is not lo\nAs recorded: ¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)", "response": "¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)", "expected": "(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 271, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "5c172812", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "meta", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "eaae534dbfcc", "prof_total_s": 371.3, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 371.3, "prof_tok_in": 466, "prof_tok_out": 14, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
